/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  compaction02 -- A BRITTLE EXCIPIENT ON THE COMPACTION BENCH: DICALCIUM
  PHOSPHATE, AND WHY IT IS NOT LACTOSE.

  The donepezil tablet case (tutorials/batch/plant/donepezil01_tablets) is
  62 % lactose monohydrate, a FRAGMENTING excipient.  This case was meant
  to fit the compaction models to open lactose data, and it does not,
  because NO open dataset with tabulated lactose pressure-density or
  strength-porosity rows was found on 2026-11-02 (CC BY sources searched:
  MDPI Pharmaceutics, Int. J. Pharm. X, PMC open access, Zenodo, Data in
  Brief; the lactose datasets located were NonCommercial, figure-only, or
  "available on request").  No lactose numbers were invented in its place.

  What it fits instead is the open brittle reference that WAS found, from
  the same two sources as compaction01, so the plastic-against-brittle
  contrast can be read off two cases built the same way:

    dcpd_inDie       DCPD Emcompress premium (dihydrate), van der Haven et
                     al., Sci. Data 13 (2026) 1468, CC BY 4.0: 42 tablets,
                     in-die density at maximum force; 19 crushed
                     diametrally.  Compared with the sigma_0 and k_b the
                     dataset itself reports.
    dcpa_lubricated  CaHPO4 anhydrous DI-CAFOS A60 + 2 % sodium stearyl
                     fumarate + 0.3 % silica (Kukuls & Mohylyuk 2025, CC BY
                     4.0; protocol of Mohylyuk et al., Pharmaceutics 16
                     (2024) 362), out of die, compared with that paper's
                     Py = 434 MPa.

  WHAT TO READ.  Py here is several hundred MPa -- several times MCC's
  (compaction01) -- the signature of a material that densifies by
  FRACTURE, not by flow.  Its tablets are weak at a given porosity (low
  tensile strength) and k_b is large: the strength collapses fast as the
  pores open.  Lactose sits between the two: for Tablettose 80 the
  literature gives an in-die Py of 120.8 +/- 1.5 MPa at 11.28 mm, against
  52.2 +/- 4.2 MPa for MCC Vivapur 102 in the same study (Lura et al., Int.
  J. Pharm. X 2 (2020) 100061, Table 2, CC BY) -- quoted for the donepezil
  group to compare with, NOT fitted here, because the study gives the
  curves only as figures.

  A FINDING, PRINTED AND NOT TUNED: on both materials of the van der Haven
  dataset (here and in the other powder case) the fitted k_b agrees with
  the dataset's own constant within 10 %, but the fitted sigma_0 comes out
  about 1.5 times the dataset's.  A fit in sigma_t instead of ln(sigma_t)
  does not close it (measured: 30.9 and 28.4 MPa).  The files read do not
  say how the dataset's "Eq. 4" was fitted or on which porosity; its report
  PDF was not read.  sigma_0 is an extrapolation to zero porosity, so a
  small difference in slope or in the porosity basis moves it a lot.

  FOR THE DONEPEZIL GROUP.  To fit your own lactose: find an open dataset
  (or digitise a CC BY figure, stating the reading error in the dataset
  header), write it in the grammar of the files in constant/experimental/,
  declare its Heckel window with a reason, and put the paper's own Py in
  `reported {}`.  Say which lactose (monohydrate milled, sieved,
  agglomerated, spray-dried, anhydrous): Py differs between grades by a
  factor of two.  A wet-granulated blend (your case's route) compacts
  differently again from the dry excipient, and NO mixture rule is
  implemented -- the excipient stands in for the blend, and you say so.

  THE PAPER'S Py AND THIS CASE'S.  Mohylyuk et al. 2024 analyse the F 0-100
  formulation between 250 and 450 MPa.  The deposited averages have three
  rows there (243 MPa falls just below it); a 3-point line refuses here, so
  the case declares 140-440 MPa (four rows) and both values are printed.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        dcpd_inDie;
        type        compactionFit;
        material    "DCPD Emcompress premium (in-die, van der Haven et al. 2026)";
        dataset     "constant/experimental/vanderHaven2026_dcpd_inDie.dat";
        //  The dataset's own EFFECTIVE true density (Kuentz-Leuenberger fit).
        trueDensity 2.438 g/cm3;

        heckel
        {
            Pmin    100 MPa;
            Pmax    450 MPa;
            reason  "the middle of the range: below ~100 MPa the fragments are still rearranging (the curve is steeper), above ~450 MPa it bends upwards again";
        }
        kawakita
        {
            Pmin    25 MPa;
            Pmax    710 MPa;
            reason  "P/C is straight over the whole range the dataset covers";
        }
        ryshkewitch
        {
            dataset "constant/experimental/vanderHaven2026_dcpd_diametral.dat";
        }
        reported
        {
            sigma0  20.5 MPa;
            kb      15.0;
            source  "van der Haven et al. 2026, Zenodo archive, Processed data/Single/Ryshkewitch-Duckworth constants.csv, 100 wt% DCPD, diametrical";
        }
    }
    {
        name        dcpa_lubricated;
        type        compactionFit;
        material    "CaHPO4 DI-CAFOS A60 + 2 % PRUV + 0.3 % Syloid (out-of-die, Kukuls & Mohylyuk 2025)";
        dataset     "constant/experimental/kukuls2025_F_0-100.dat";
        heckel
        {
            Pmin    140 MPa;
            Pmax    440 MPa;
            reason  "four rows; the paper's 250-450 MPa window holds three deposited rows; the highest pressure's solid fraction falls (78.1 % after 79.3 %, s.d. 4 %)";
        }
        kawakita
        {
            Pmin    90 MPa;
            Pmax    480 MPa;
            reason  "all six main compressions (in-die strain)";
        }
        ryshkewitch { }
        reported
        {
            Py      434 MPa;
            source  "Mohylyuk et al., Pharmaceutics 16 (2024) 362, section 3 (out-of-die Heckel, F 0-100)";
        }
    }
);
